# Naming and Explicit Parameters

## Names describe intent, not mechanism

```python
# BAD
def handle(data):
def process(obj):
def run(x):

# GOOD
def get_health_status(provider: HealthStatusProvider) -> HealthStatus:
def create_user_account(user_data: CreateUserDTO) -> UserAccount:
def cancel_pending_order(order_id: OrderId, reason: CancellationReason) -> CancelledOrder:
```

## Explicit parameters — always pass by keyword when ambiguous

```python
# BAD
status = HealthStatus(True, "ok")

# GOOD
status = HealthStatus(is_alive=True, message="ok")
```

Boolean arguments are always keyword-only:

```python
def send_notification(*, send_copy_to_admin: bool) -> None: ...
```

## No magic values

```python
# BAD
if status == 2:

# GOOD
if status == OrderStatus.CONFIRMED:
```

## Collections and optionals are named clearly

```python
# BAD
items: list
d: dict

# GOOD
pending_orders: list[Order]
user_by_email: dict[str, User]
found_user: User | None
```
